# Project Context

This project is a platform for planning and organizing LinkedIn content.

The purpose of the platform is to help users:

- Plan what they want to post on LinkedIn
- Decide when content should be published
- Organize post ideas
- Draft LinkedIn posts
- See upcoming posts in a calendar or timeline
- Track the status of each post
- Keep an overview of their LinkedIn content strategy

# Core Data Model

A LinkedIn post should normally contain:

- title
- content
- scheduledDate
- status
- category
- notes
- createdAt
- updatedAt

Possible post statuses:

- idea
- draft
- ready
- scheduled
- published

Do not invent additional fields unless they are needed for a feature.

# Main Features

The platform should support:

- Creating a new LinkedIn post
- Editing a post
- Deleting a post
- Saving posts as drafts
- Setting a publication date
- Changing post status
- Viewing upcoming posts
- Viewing posts in a calendar
- Filtering posts by status
- Filtering posts by date
- Organizing content ideas

# UI Principles

The interface should feel:

- Simple
- Calm
- Professional
- Modern
- Easy to scan

Prioritize usability over decorative design.

Avoid unnecessary complexity.

Use clear spacing and visual hierarchy.

Important actions should be easy to find.

# Dashboard

The dashboard should give the user a quick overview of their LinkedIn content.

Prefer showing:

- Upcoming posts
- Draft posts
- Scheduled posts
- Recently published posts
- A simple calendar or timeline
- Quick action for creating a new post

# Post Creation

Creating a LinkedIn post should be fast.

The user should be able to enter:

- A title for internal organization
- The LinkedIn post content
- A planned publication date
- A status
- Optional notes or category

Do not require unnecessary fields.

# Calendar

The calendar should help users understand when they plan to publish content.

Posts should be clearly associated with their scheduled date.

The user should be able to identify:

- What is planned
- What is ready
- What has already been published

# LinkedIn Content

When generating example LinkedIn content:

- Keep the writing natural
- Avoid excessive emojis
- Avoid fake engagement bait
- Avoid overly corporate language
- Prefer short readable paragraphs
- Use clear hooks when appropriate
- Keep posts useful and relevant
- Do not invent personal experiences for the user

# Code Style

- Use TypeScript
- Prefer React functional components
- Keep components focused and reasonably small
- Extract reusable components when useful
- Avoid unnecessary abstractions
- Use descriptive variable and function names
- Prefer async/await
- Keep business logic separate from presentation logic when practical

# React / Next.js

When using Next.js:

- Prefer the App Router
- Use Server Components by default
- Add "use client" only when client-side state, effects, browser APIs, or event handlers are required
- Keep API keys and secrets server-side
- Never expose secret environment variables to the client

# Styling

- Use Tailwind CSS
- Prefer existing design patterns in the project
- Keep styling consistent
- Avoid inline styles unless necessary
- Prefer responsive layouts
- Ensure forms and buttons have clear hover, focus, and disabled states

# Forms

Forms should:

- Have clear labels or placeholders
- Validate required fields
- Prevent accidental duplicate submissions
- Show loading states when submitting
- Show useful errors when something fails

# Dates

Dates are important in this project.

Always:

- Store dates in a consistent format
- Be careful with time zones
- Separate display formatting from stored date values
- Avoid ambiguous date formats where possible

# Safety

Never:

- Expose API keys
- Put secrets in client components
- Delete user content without an explicit user action
- Silently overwrite existing content

# When Editing Existing Code

Before making changes:

1. Read the existing implementation.
2. Follow existing patterns where reasonable.
3. Make the smallest change that solves the problem.
4. Avoid rewriting unrelated code.
5. Preserve existing functionality unless the task explicitly changes it.

# Priorities

When several solutions are possible, prioritize:

1. Correctness
2. Simplicity
3. Good user experience
4. Maintainability
5. Performance
